  elsevier3200944Abstract:
    'Recently it has been proposed that comparing the net-baryon (B) stopping with net-electric charge (Q) stopping can help studies of the baryon stopping mechanism in nuclear collisions. Here we find the B/Q\u{202F}×\u{202F}Z/A ratio to be very sensitive to the difference between s and <math altimg="si1.svg"><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> quark rapidity distributions. For mid-rapidity of isobar collisions at 200A GeV, a multi-phase transport (AMPT) model gives slightly more <math altimg="si1.svg"><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></math> than s, which leads to B/Q\u{202F}×\u{202F}Z/A\u{202F}<\u{202F}1, while the model without the <math altimg="si2.svg"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo linebreak="goodbreak">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></math> asymmetry gives B/Q\u{202F}×\u{202F}Z/A\u{202F}≥\u{202F}1. Comparing Ru+Ru and Zr+Zr isobar collisions, the AMPT (and UrQMD) model gives B/ΔQ\u{202F}×\u{202F}ΔZ/A\u{202F}<\u{202F}1 at mid-rapidity at all centralities, which strongly contradicts the recent STAR data. We also find that the B/ΔQ\u{202F}×\u{202F}ΔZ/A ratio is very sensitive to the net-light quark (u, d) stoppings, but it is less sensitive to the <math altimg="si2.svg"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo linebreak="goodbreak">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></math> asymmetry than the B/Q\u{202F}×\u{202F}Z/A ratio by a factor of 3. These results are expected to help us resolve the stopping puzzle and better understand the baryon stopping mechanism in the future.',
